{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And he shall wash the one being purified, etc.</b> One must understand what this washing is for, since he still renders garments impure during the days of his counting? And also, according to what I explained (Leviticus 14:7), that this impurity for which he washes his garments on its account is the cessation of the tzara'at, as I inferred from the verse's saying ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת״ — nevertheless he would not wash his garments while he still renders them impure. And in Torat Kohanim I found that they said, and this is their language: ״וְכִבֶּס״ — what does it come to teach? If to teach that he renders garments impure by contact, it is an a fortiori inference from the days of his counting, etc. Rather, it is one thing to render lying and sitting [impure] and one thing to render [impure] by contact — up to here. According to this, <b>וְכִבֶּס</b> etc. comes to say that the impurity of lying and sitting has ceased. Now, the primary necessity of the derivation of Torat Kohanim is only from the force of our difficulty, and they were not required to explain it on the level of its plain sense, for without our difficulty there is no necessity for them to say ״what does it come to teach״ as a necessity — for who told us that it comes to teach? Perhaps it comes for its own sake, that he must wash his garments on the day he is purified from the tzara'at and on the day of completing the count of seven days, just as we find that HaShem commanded him to shave all his hair and to wash all his flesh on the day he is purified and at the end of the days of counting — likewise HaShem commanded regarding the washing. Rather, surely the core of their difficulty is that he would not wash if he still renders garments impure, and therefore you must say that it did not come except to teach a thing, and therefore they said: what does it come to teach, if to teach, etc. — it is an a fortiori inference, and he answered, etc." "<b>And I saw</b> that our Rabbi Hillel wrote to emend the reading of ״to render lying and sitting impure,״ on the strength of what they said in Eilu Devarim (Pesachim 67:) that a zav is more stringent than a metzora, for he renders lying and sitting impure, and causes impurity by entry [into a space containing him]. And according to his words, one must emend several baraitot as well that come there in Torat Kohanim, which teach that the metzora renders lying and sitting impure. And the Rav, the author of Korban Aharon, already rejected his words with a rejection that cannot be relied upon. And what they said in Eilu Devarim, that the zav is more stringent than the metzora — so it is according to the truth: during the days of his counting, a zav renders lying and sitting impure, while during the days of counting of a metzora he does not render lying and sitting impure. And this is the very thing we learned from the verse ״וְכִבֶּס הַמִּטַּהֵר״ stated twice, and so the Rambam and all the decisors ruled, and our Rabbi Hillel did not aim at the correct view in this." "<b>And he shall wash in water, etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said, and this is their language: ״בַּמַּיִם״ — even in the waters of a mikveh; and is it not an a fortiori inference: a zav, who does not require sprinkling of living water, does require immersion in living water — a metzora, who does require [sprinkling], etc.! The verse therefore says ״וְרָחַץ בַּמַּיִם״ — even, etc. — up to here. The plain sense of their words is that they derive the superfluity of the word ״בַּמַּיִם,״ since it should not have needed to say anything but ״וְרָחַץ,״ and so the author of Korban Ahron explained. And it is difficult: but the verse has already excluded immersion in living water for the metzora in the parashah of the zav, as is taught there (Leviticus 15:13) in Torat Kohanim: ״וְרָחַץ בְּשָׂרוֹ בְּמַיִם חַיִּים״ — the zav requires living water, and the metzora does not require living water; and is it not an a fortiori inference: if the zav, etc. — the verse therefore says ״בְּשָׂרוֹ בְּמַיִם חַיִּים״ — the zav requires, etc., and the metzora does not require, etc. — up to here. This implies that [the exclusion] is derived from the superfluity of the word ״בְּשָׂרוֹ,״ and so the Rav, the author of Korban Ahron, explained. If so, why was it necessary to say ״בַּמַּיִם״? And it is possible to say that the core of the Tanna's derivation is from the superfluity of the word ״בַּמַּיִם״ — from it he derives to include the waters of a mikveh. And the word ״בְּשָׂרוֹ״ was needed to exclude his garments, as is also derived there in Torat Kohanim. And what he also attached to it as an exclusion of living water for the metzora, [was done] incidentally, and he relied on the superfluity of the word ״בַּמַּיִם.״ And with this it is no difficulty why he derives two derivations from the word ״בְּשָׂרוֹ.״ And the author of Korban Ahron explained that he derives from the fact that it did not say ״וְרָחַץ הַזָּב״ but said ״בְּשָׂרוֹ,״ but according to our words we have no need for this." "And what they derived in Torat Kohanim in parashat Tzav, in the verse (Leviticus 6:21) ״וּמֹרַק וְשֻׁטַּף בַּמָּיִם״ said regarding a vessel in which a sin-offering was cooked — and this is their language: ״וְשֻׁטַּף״ — one might think [it requires] forty se'ah; the verse therefore says ״בַּמָּיִם״ — any amount [suffices]; ״בַּמָּיִם״ and not in wine and not in diluted [wine] — up to here. One should not compare the superfluity of ״בַּמָּיִם״ said regarding the sin-offering to the superfluity of ״בַּמַּיִם״ said here. For there, if it had not said ״בַּמָּיִם,״ I would have required only forty se'ah of mikveh water, since we have no reason to say that it requires living water so that the verse comes to exclude it — and we are forced to say that it comes to exclude the very requirement of forty se'ah. This is not so regarding ״בַּמַּיִם״ said here: since there is reason to say that living water is required, and ״בַּמַּיִם״ comes as a superfluity, we set it aside to exclude living water. And one who would come to say that it excludes forty se'ah must bring proof from where. And what he added there to derive ״בַּמָּיִם״ and not in wine, etc. — this is only from the meaning [of the word] and not from the superfluity. And see the verse after this, where the matter will be resolved in a different manner."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The phrase דִּיַּקְתִּי ('I inferred/examined closely') is rendered as 'I inferred,' preserving the investigative sense the Or HaChaim uses when referring back to his own prior analysis.", 1 "The reference to רבנו הלל and the author of קרבן אהרן follows standard identification of these commentators; 'the Rav, the author of Korban Aharon' is rendered literally as the Or HaChaim's form of address.", 2 "The Or HaChaim attributes one explanation of the double derivation from בשרו to בעל קרבן אהרן, but the final clause 'ולדברינו אין אנו צריכין לזה' indicates he prefers his own resolution over that of Korban Ahron.", 3 "The phrase 'ושדינן ליה למעט מים חיים' is an Aramaic construction meaning 'we set it aside to exclude living water,' rendered literally to preserve the talmudic register of the argumentation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:55.102849331Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}